<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu'tazilism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu'tazilism was a school of Islamic theology that emphasised reason and rational argument in interpreting doctrine, including the view that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was created rather than eternal. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> it was adopted as state doctrine, asserting the caliph's right to define theological orthodoxy. The resulting inquisition and its later abandonment shaped relations between rulers and religious scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
